WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.285
What is the most misunderstood idea in physics?

00:00:03.285 --> 00:00:06.159
This was proof that nature is non-local.

00:00:06.159 --> 00:00:10.241
If you ask physicists, what's the lesson of the 2022 Nobel Prize?

00:00:10.241 --> 00:00:12.795
"Oh, it's because of the non-locality of physics."

00:00:12.795 --> 00:00:15.186
Is there faster than light communication?

00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:18.822
Whenever we have evidence of what looks like magic, like here,

00:00:18.822 --> 00:00:22.760
violating the Bell inequality with such a big winning rate, is evidence that there's

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.588
something we have not yet understood. Everett was in the business of applying physics

00:00:26.588 --> 00:00:31.580
to the whole thing. Trying to build with classical Lego blocks quantum mechanics,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.230
this is promised to fail.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.880
Yes, yes. What's the alternative?

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.940
Here we go. I hope every competent physicist would be extremely critical of the account

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:41.847
I've given.

00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:42.753
Hi Charles.

00:00:42.753 --> 00:00:45.825
Hi Maxime, thank you for the invitation.

00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:49.288
What is the most misunderstood idea in physics?

00:00:49.288 --> 00:00:58.805
Well, I think that since Bell's theorem and the experiments that vindicated the violation

00:00:58.805 --> 00:01:04.908
of Bell inequalities, especially with the hype that all of this research program got

00:01:04.908 --> 00:01:11.620
with the 2022 Nobel Prize in Physics awarded to Aspect, Clauser and Zeilinger for establishing

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.624
that nature does indeed violate Bell inequalities, I think that the most misunderstood

00:01:16.624 --> 00:01:21.176
idea in physics, or at least in the physics community around which I revolve,

00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:24.300
is that this was proof that nature is non-local.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.460
There's a lot of things to unpack, and I guess it's setting up the topic for a conversation here.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.363
We would need to unpack the hypotheses of Bell's theorem, what it means to violate a

00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:40.313
Bell inequality, what happens when we really see that indeed photons can violate Bell

00:01:40.313 --> 00:01:43.389
inequalities, or other quantum systems when they're entangled,

00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:49.401
and why do people conclude that, "Oh, this is therefore the only viable explanation is

00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:52.880
that we have non-local action," or that the world is non-local,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.440
or however this could be put.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.760
The explanation that remains fully local is basically the one that trusts quantum theory

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.467
in the first place. So I've launched a few cues: wherever you want to rebound,

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.260
we can start from the beginning, it's up to you.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:11.260
But I would say that the most misconceived idea is this idea that physics is non-local

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.180
because of violation of Bell inequalities.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.140
Who are you as a physicist? What's your profile?

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.488
Right. I'm a postdoctoral researcher at École de technologie supérieure,

00:02:20.488 --> 00:02:26.140
so I'm doing a postdoc with Claude Crépeau, who's my advisor and friend.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.660
It's funny because I keep following cryptographers.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.620
I was working with a cryptographer, Stefan Wolf, in Lugano before,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:38.997
and my PhD advisor is Gilles Brassard, who's well known for his work in quantum information

00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:44.140
theory and quantum cryptography, but he was also by training a cryptographer.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.560
I'm fascinated by cryptography. There's a lot of things to unpack here.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.820
But my heart in research has always been in the foundations of physics.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.987
And it turns out that the great cryptographers I had the chance to overlap with were

00:02:57.987 --> 00:03:04.200
always giving me not only freedom to explore these ideas, but also plenty of very interesting inputs.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.762
So that's my background, but I'd say I'm a theoretical physicist and also a theoretical

00:03:09.762 --> 00:03:15.960
computer scientist, mostly interested in fundamental aspects of physics and information theory.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.113
What are Bell inequalities? I mean, they're named after Bell,

00:03:20.113 --> 00:03:23.720
a well-known physicist, but what are the Bell inequalities?

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.780
Bell's theorem was put forth in 1964. For this audience, right,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.360
I believe this audience is more generally interested in physics without being an expert.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.040
I would not recommend delving into Bell's paper.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.800
It contains integrals, and also the conclusion is not extremely sharp.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.323
You see the inequality and you see the fact that quantum theory predicts the violation

00:03:45.323 --> 00:03:48.624
of these inequalities. But nowadays we have easier versions.

00:03:48.624 --> 00:03:54.063
Notably, one of them is due to CHSH, and it's generally presented as a game.

00:03:54.063 --> 00:03:58.237
So if you want, I can take you on board with what is the CHSH game,

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:03.241
how is it played, and how do we get this surprising news that quantum mechanics permits

00:04:03.241 --> 00:04:04.890
to violate a Bell inequality.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.740
I'm drawing my explanations that I did in this book.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.079
So this chapter in Bold Conjectures, Volume Two.

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:17.335
It's a great volume put forth by the Conjecture Institute, edited by Logan Chipkin,

00:04:17.335 --> 00:04:20.441
and I contributed to a chapter. It's dedicated to a wide audience.

00:04:20.441 --> 00:04:23.698
So it's funny because I'm going to follow you through this argument here,

00:04:23.698 --> 00:04:28.049
and I believe it's a crisp way and vivid way to explain it.

00:04:28.049 --> 00:04:31.516
So the CHSH game is played between two players cooperatively.

00:04:31.516 --> 00:04:36.444
Let's name our players Alice and Bob, and they're initially co-located.

00:04:36.444 --> 00:04:43.398
They know the rules of the game, and they can initially agree on a strategy to beat the

00:04:43.398 --> 00:04:46.648
game, to try to achieve the highest possible score.

00:04:46.648 --> 00:04:51.811
Agree on a strategy, it means they could exchange pieces of information if they want.

00:04:51.811 --> 00:04:54.634
They can agree on some fixed instruction sets.

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:58.371
They can also agree that the instruction sets will change at some time.

00:04:58.371 --> 00:05:02.839
They could do whatever they want to build a strategy to beat the game.

00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:05.740
However, when they'll be playing, they'll be separated.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:13.420
I like to think of Alice is going to Mercury and Bob is going to a moon around Jupiter.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:19.260
So that the isolation is an isolation in space, to make sure that they cannot communicate.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.193
They can't cheat, they can't exchange messages while playing the game.

00:05:22.193 --> 00:05:27.744
The game, they will receive a list of questions, similar to a quiz that one might receive,

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:32.108
with a list of, say, 40 questions. And each question is a binary question,

00:05:32.108 --> 00:05:36.940
so it's kind of helpful to think of the question as being either zero or one.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:41.309
And then they must reply to each question an answer which is also binary,

00:05:41.309 --> 00:05:42.366
so zero or one.

00:05:42.366 --> 00:05:45.274
And I haven't yet told you the rules of the game.

00:05:45.274 --> 00:05:48.920
So what are the rules of the game? They're as follows.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.864
If one of the questions in the question pair contains a zero,

00:05:53.864 --> 00:05:58.198
Alice and Bob must try to produce the same answer.

00:05:58.198 --> 00:06:02.180
So, for example, you might be Alice, I'm Bob.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:08.130
You receive a 0, I receive a 1 on, say, the question round number 17.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.820
We have to try to output the same answer.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:11.320
Yes.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:16.329
And so, for example, if you output 0 and I output 0, we would mark a point for that round.

00:06:16.329 --> 00:06:23.237
However, when it's not the case that one of the two questions contains a zero,

00:06:23.237 --> 00:06:26.569
then Alice and Bob must try to output different bits.

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:29.809
So just to recap so far, we have two people.

00:06:29.809 --> 00:06:35.233
One of them is on some far away planet, the other on some other far away planet,

00:06:35.233 --> 00:06:41.335
and depending on the number in the question, they need to output the same answer or different answer.

00:06:41.335 --> 00:06:47.668
Correct. So for a given round, say the first question, if the question pair contains

00:06:47.668 --> 00:06:51.924
a zero, Alice and Bob must try to produce the same answer.

00:06:51.924 --> 00:06:59.363
11 would be valid, 00 would be valid, but 01 would not be valid if they produce 01 and

00:06:59.363 --> 00:07:01.626
they did indeed contain a 0 as a question.

00:07:01.626 --> 00:07:05.905
However, if they both receive a 1, then they must try to produce different answers.

00:07:05.905 --> 00:07:12.793
If we only play the CHSH game once, we don't learn much, but I like to think of this

00:07:12.793 --> 00:07:17.340
as they receive 40 questions, and so does Bob, and these questions,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.797
you can think of them as being drawn at random, or produced by a process that Alice and

00:07:21.797 --> 00:07:25.399
Bob did not have access to when they were initially co-located.

00:07:25.399 --> 00:07:28.370
When they initially prepare their strategies, when they're together,

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:32.300
they don't know which questions they'll be asked, and that's part of the difficulty.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:32.800
Okay.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.384
And I think it's good now to pause to really absorb the game,

00:07:36.384 --> 00:07:39.620
to put ourselves back in Alice's and Bob's shoes.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.030
While they know the rules of the game and they're initially co-located,

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:47.320
what would be, you think, some strategy they could come up with?

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.265
They could technically have an agreement beforehand, right, before heading to the planet.

00:07:51.265 --> 00:07:53.454
So they want to maximize their score, right?

00:07:53.454 --> 00:07:56.932
The rule is such that if one of the question pair contains a zero,

00:07:56.932 --> 00:08:05.113
they must output the same bit. And note that on average three quarters of the question

00:08:05.113 --> 00:08:10.558
pairs will contain a zero. It's kind of helpful to think of the questions that they'll

00:08:10.558 --> 00:08:13.196
receive as being sampled randomly and identically distributed,

00:08:13.196 --> 00:08:17.980
but note it will not be required for carrying out the experiment.

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:22.460
Yeah. And it's three-fourths because it's either 00, 10, 01 or 11.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:26.628
Correct, exactly. There's a lot of ways in which a question pair can contain a zero.

00:08:26.628 --> 00:08:27.128
Yes.

00:08:27.128 --> 00:08:29.996
You named the three ways. So only if the question pair is 11,

00:08:29.996 --> 00:08:31.696
then they need to output different bits.

00:08:31.696 --> 00:08:32.196
Yes.

00:08:32.196 --> 00:08:35.962
So you might think, let's always output zero.

00:08:35.962 --> 00:08:36.462
Yes.

00:08:36.462 --> 00:08:39.447
That's a common strategy that makes a lot of sense.

00:08:39.447 --> 00:08:44.289
Alice and Bob, they will leave the planet Earth toward their station knowing that whenever

00:08:44.289 --> 00:08:52.344
the question contains a zero, so either 00, 01, or 10, they'll mark a point on that round.

00:08:52.344 --> 00:08:56.957
But whenever the question pair will be 11, since they both produce zeros,

00:08:56.957 --> 00:09:00.837
and they need to produce different outputs, they will not have a point on those rounds.

00:09:00.837 --> 00:09:04.299
And they should expect roughly a winning rate of 30 out of 40.

00:09:04.299 --> 00:09:10.000
And it's actually easy to prove that that's the case for the always output 00 strategy,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.677
if we then assume again a distribution on the questions, which is very helpful to think

00:09:15.677 --> 00:09:16.270
this way.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:21.383
But we're not done. We only proved that that strategy achieves three quarter winning rate.

00:09:21.383 --> 00:09:24.772
Obviously, they could also decide to output one all the time,

00:09:24.772 --> 00:09:29.075
and that doesn't change anything. They'll always mark a point whenever the question contains

00:09:29.075 --> 00:09:33.964
a zero, and they'll not mark a point when the question pair is 11.

00:09:33.964 --> 00:09:41.340
But we can start formalizing this a little bit, and one first step that is kind of helpful

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.400
in the formalization is to study these deterministic strategies.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.320
When Alice receives her question, her output is going to be a function of the question.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.340
The deterministic strategy that we established, where the output function was the constant

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:58.868
zero: Alice, whichever input she received, she outputs zero.

00:09:58.868 --> 00:10:03.555
That's a function, always zero. But she could have said, "You know what,

00:10:03.555 --> 00:10:06.930
I'm going to copy the question. If the question is zero, my answer is zero."

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:07.430
Yeah.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.720
"If the question is one, my answer is one."

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:10.220
Yeah.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:13.112
Or another deterministic function would be, "I'm going to flip the question.

00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:14.796
If the question is zero, I'll output one.

00:10:14.796 --> 00:10:18.851
If the question is one, I'll output zero." Or another deterministic function is the constant

00:10:18.851 --> 00:10:22.133
one: regardless of the question, I'm going to output one.

00:10:22.133 --> 00:10:25.627
And you're going to go, "Well, Charles, are you going forever enumerating the functions

00:10:25.627 --> 00:10:30.513
that Alice could possibly think of?" Well, no, I'm done.

00:10:30.513 --> 00:10:36.140
There aren't that many. Because it's a function of one bit, the domain is one bit,

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:40.402
so it has two possibilities, into one bit, it has two possibilities of outcomes.

00:10:40.402 --> 00:10:43.940
You can list them all, and there's only four such functions.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:44.940
Yes.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:48.589
And we've already listed them all. And therefore the local deterministic functions for

00:10:48.589 --> 00:10:52.566
Alice, because Alice's response function, she doesn't know about Bob's input,

00:10:52.566 --> 00:10:54.840
so her function cannot depend on Bob's input.

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:55.840
Yeah.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.840
Right? It's solely on her input.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:58.840
On her input, yeah.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.420
There's only four possible local functions for Alice and only four possible local functions

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:06.416
for Bob. And therefore, there are 16 common strategies.

00:11:06.416 --> 00:11:13.782
And the both players always output zero strategy was one such strategy among the 16 joint strategies.

00:11:13.782 --> 00:11:19.587
But then we can start thinking, "Oh, what if Alice always outputs zero and Bob copies

00:11:19.587 --> 00:11:23.596
the question?" And then we can start mismatching these, right?

00:11:23.596 --> 00:11:26.424
We vary the four possibilities for Alice's deterministic functions,

00:11:26.424 --> 00:11:33.120
same for Bob. It's not so much work, but with a little work we realize that eight such

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.486
joint strategies give you a winning rate of three quarters.

00:11:36.486 --> 00:11:39.446
You're always stuck on some question pair.

00:11:39.446 --> 00:11:39.946
Yes.

00:11:39.946 --> 00:11:44.511
And eight strategies are way worse. They give you a winning rate of one quarter.

00:11:44.511 --> 00:11:45.011
Yes.

00:11:45.011 --> 00:11:47.816
So suppose you're smart, you're Alice and Bob, you kind of realize that,

00:11:47.816 --> 00:11:50.148
okay, so the always zero strategy was pretty good.

00:11:50.148 --> 00:11:52.152
We have a winning rate of three quarters.

00:11:52.152 --> 00:11:55.716
To give you an intuition of how this works, the thing that's changing,

00:11:55.716 --> 00:12:00.473
if we change to another strategy that has a winning rate of three quarters,

00:12:00.473 --> 00:12:04.991
Alice and Bob will get the wrong answer on a different input.

00:12:04.991 --> 00:12:09.621
For example, you could secure the fact that they saved the 1-1 scenario.

00:12:09.621 --> 00:12:14.720
Whenever the question is 1-1, they output in such a way that it will give them the right answer.

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:20.834
But then you can follow the chain of logic, which guarantees that they will get stuck

00:12:20.834 --> 00:12:26.551
on one question. Basically, in any scenario, as we've seen so far at least,

00:12:26.551 --> 00:12:30.504
you cannot get over three quarters of the answers right.

00:12:30.504 --> 00:12:36.040
So far, the scenarios we've investigated are those where Alice has a deterministic strategy.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:42.655
I call that a deterministic strategy, meaning that her answer is a function of her question.

00:12:42.655 --> 00:12:45.013
Yeah, it's fixed based on the input that she gets.

00:12:45.013 --> 00:12:49.080
Right. I basically gave an argument. You exhaustively search through these 16 deterministic

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.880
strategies, and you make the table, and look for yourself: eight of them have a winning

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:55.743
rate of three quarters, eight of them have a winning rate of one quarter.

00:12:55.743 --> 00:13:00.741
And now, one could say, "But that's not the most general thing Alice and Bob could do.

00:13:00.741 --> 00:13:05.799
They could answer at whim, just for fun." But know that if they start doing that,

00:13:05.799 --> 00:13:11.593
what they'll be doing is mixing randomly some of the response functions that are already

00:13:11.593 --> 00:13:13.923
encompassed in those deterministic strategies, right?

00:13:13.923 --> 00:13:17.768
For example, if you say she's just tossing a coin, and if it's heads,

00:13:17.768 --> 00:13:20.620
she's going to answer zero, if it's tails, she's going to answer one,

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.913
regardless of the question. Well, that's an example of just mixing two of the deterministic

00:13:23.913 --> 00:13:26.927
strategies we've showed, the constant zero and the constant one,

00:13:26.927 --> 00:13:33.191
with randomness. But if you start mixing randomly strategies that are bounded by a winning

00:13:33.191 --> 00:13:34.353
rate of three quarters...

00:13:34.353 --> 00:13:40.580
Yes, it's not going to help. You're still stuck in this ceiling of three quarters.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:43.820
You can't do better than the deterministic approach.

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:46.624
Okay. So there's always a caveat where you could get lucky.

00:13:46.624 --> 00:13:47.124
Yeah.

00:13:47.124 --> 00:13:51.807
And your coin flip was aligned with the question in such a way that you replied good

00:13:51.807 --> 00:13:55.087
answers for the first few trials, but eventually you'll get stuck.

00:13:55.087 --> 00:13:57.540
Yeah, statistically, with a large enough sample.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.769
Yeah, exactly. You increase the sample size.

00:14:01.769 --> 00:14:02.420
Answering randomly.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.700
And then you go, "Oh, but there's other things they could do." For example,

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.128
I said there's a list of 40 questions, I guess it's 40 question rounds.

00:14:08.128 --> 00:14:13.131
Maybe they decide that on the even questions they do the always zero strategy.

00:14:13.131 --> 00:14:13.631
Right.

00:14:13.631 --> 00:14:16.048
And on the odd questions, they do something else.

00:14:16.048 --> 00:14:17.300
Yeah.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:22.100
But that would yet be just another way of mixing strategies that are capped at a three-quarter

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:26.756
winning rate. And so they cannot expect to beat the three-quarter ceiling.

00:14:26.756 --> 00:14:29.159
So we're walking towards Bell's theorem.

00:14:29.159 --> 00:14:36.603
We actually have one half of Bell's theorem at the moment, or the CHSH version of Bell's theorem.

00:14:36.603 --> 00:14:39.929
This half is the ceiling, the Bell inequality.

00:14:39.929 --> 00:14:44.953
The three-quarter bound that we've just been discussing is called a Bell inequality.

00:14:44.953 --> 00:14:52.402
What it means is that all of these ways we can conceive of, of Alice discussing with

00:14:52.402 --> 00:14:56.198
Bob, exchanging classical pieces of information, exchanging instruction sets,

00:14:56.198 --> 00:15:00.922
like, "Oh, on even questions, do that, and if the question is...,

00:15:00.922 --> 00:15:08.316
do that," all of these ways of trying to beat the CHSH game are bounded statistically

00:15:08.316 --> 00:15:13.027
by three quarters. So that's the three quarters, the Bell inequality.

00:15:13.027 --> 00:15:20.374
And the other half of Bell's theorem is, I don't want to spoil what it is,

00:15:20.374 --> 00:15:28.159
I'll let you jump inside my story to make you think of the wonder of what it gives.

00:15:28.159 --> 00:15:32.416
Suppose you see Alice and Bob playing. They're discussing on Earth,

00:15:32.416 --> 00:15:37.535
and you see them exchange some stuff before they leave.

00:15:37.535 --> 00:15:42.659
Before Alice goes to Mercury, she carries some form of crystal,

00:15:42.659 --> 00:15:47.263
and Bob also carries a form of crystal when he's on Jupiter.

00:15:47.263 --> 00:15:50.927
You might think, okay, maybe they're superstitious and they think maybe this crystal

00:15:50.927 --> 00:15:57.419
might help them win the Bell test. And then they go back after the test and they score

00:15:57.419 --> 00:16:04.018
34 out of 40. Not bad. Those crystals, maybe they got lucky,

00:16:04.018 --> 00:16:04.518
right?

00:16:04.518 --> 00:16:08.662
So you go again and you say, "Okay, you know what, guys, we're going to play again.

00:16:08.662 --> 00:16:13.136
But since it seems like you have remarkable abilities to play the CHSH game,

00:16:13.136 --> 00:16:18.338
next time we play, it's not 40 rounds, it's going to be 40 billion."

00:16:18.338 --> 00:16:18.952
Nice.

00:16:18.952 --> 00:16:23.727
And then they exchange just more crystals, because they need one pair of crystals per question.

00:16:23.727 --> 00:16:27.618
So in that case, you see them exchanging 40 billion crystals,

00:16:27.618 --> 00:16:32.460
one crystal for Alice, one crystal for Bob, and the first crystal corresponds to the

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.849
first crystal of Bob, and so on. They have 40 billion of them,

00:16:35.849 --> 00:16:38.705
but you don't understand. You don't know quantum mechanics.

00:16:38.705 --> 00:16:41.405
It's fine, play with crystals if you want.

00:16:41.405 --> 00:16:46.699
And then they go to their station. And obviously the setup is such that when the question

00:16:46.699 --> 00:16:54.940
sheet is given, the time that they're allowed to answer the question is such that no

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.240
signal from Alice can reach Bob's station in time.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:01.938
So five minutes, if they're really on Mercury and Jupiter, five minutes does the job.

00:17:01.938 --> 00:17:04.288
So they have five minutes to answer all the questions.

00:17:04.288 --> 00:17:08.344
Why? Because it's such that even if Alice, as soon as she gets her questions,

00:17:08.344 --> 00:17:10.360
wants to signal her questions to Bob, then...

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:12.120
It wouldn't arrive on time.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:13.971
Yeah. Bob does not have enough time.

00:17:13.971 --> 00:17:16.087
They will be declared as cheaters, or they...

00:17:16.087 --> 00:17:19.916
Yes. We will squeeze them. That's important, obviously, because I didn't make that point,

00:17:19.916 --> 00:17:23.394
but you understand that if Alice and Bob are secretly allowed to communicate...

00:17:23.394 --> 00:17:23.894
Yes.

00:17:23.894 --> 00:17:27.216
Then of course they can win this CHSH game with a hundred percent.

00:17:27.216 --> 00:17:31.140
That's why we isolate them. They're far away, you cannot communicate faster than light.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:34.165
Correct. Alice could just signal all her questions and her answers,

00:17:34.165 --> 00:17:38.024
for example, to Bob, and then Bob has all the information to match the risk and make

00:17:38.024 --> 00:17:42.227
sure he writes the right answer depending on his question, to fulfill the game's criterion.

00:17:42.227 --> 00:17:45.840
So they go with their 40 billion crystals, and they're separated across space,

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:50.700
and they come back on Earth, and they secure not 34 out of 40,

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:55.380
but they secure 30 billion-ish out of 40 billion.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:59.235
So chance is no longer a viable explanation.

00:17:59.235 --> 00:18:05.295
That is violating a Bell inequality, and that is why it's remarkable.

00:18:05.295 --> 00:18:09.358
I'm glad we're explaining this to people who are not trained in physics,

00:18:09.358 --> 00:18:14.280
because it's easy to explain, and it's easy to remark how remarkable that is.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.840
How on Earth do they do that, right?

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.360
Yes, or across Earth.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.660
Yes, exactly. Across the solar system, do they do that?

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:25.629
And those of us who are trained in quantum mechanics could look further:

00:18:25.629 --> 00:18:30.083
what do they do with their crystals? Do they just pray, or do they somehow relegate the

00:18:30.083 --> 00:18:33.260
question to the crystal? And indeed, that's what they do.

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:39.020
So if we look at how Alice and Bob achieved to win 34 billion out of 40 billion,

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:44.696
which is roughly 85% winning rate, that's what they can achieve by exchanging crystals,

00:18:44.696 --> 00:18:47.342
we should look at what they do. Arthur C.

00:18:47.342 --> 00:18:52.860
Clarke once said that any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:53.860
Yes.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:59.340
David Deutsch clarified that this is because there's no place for magic in a comprehensible universe.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:00.340
Right.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:03.655
Whenever we have evidence of what looks like magic, like here,

00:19:03.655 --> 00:19:08.435
violating the Bell inequality with such a big winning rate, is evidence that there's

00:19:08.435 --> 00:19:14.063
something we have not yet understood. And so here we are, I'll walk you through what

00:19:14.063 --> 00:19:14.760
they do.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:20.360
Alice takes her question and basically relegates the question to the crystal.

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:26.850
Inside the crystal, it could be an NV center, anything that basically acts as a quantum

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:32.115
memory for a quantum system. And when Alice and Bob were initially co-located,

00:19:32.115 --> 00:19:34.577
they prepared systems in an entangled state.

00:19:34.577 --> 00:19:40.022
These entangled states are usually presented to people as a pair of systems that share

00:19:40.022 --> 00:19:43.731
a common wave function. There's one wave function for two particles.

00:19:43.731 --> 00:19:48.029
So they prepare these 40 billion pairs of particles this way.

00:19:48.029 --> 00:19:51.278
And Alice relegates her question. So if the question is zero,

00:19:51.278 --> 00:19:54.845
what she does is that she measures her particle directly, her qubit.

00:19:54.845 --> 00:19:58.200
The particle only needs to encode a quantum bit.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:02.012
She measures it directly, and suppose I use the convention that the outcomes of qubits

00:20:02.012 --> 00:20:06.406
are either zero or one, and that's helpful because my game needs an answer which is a

00:20:06.406 --> 00:20:14.296
zero or a one. And so if the qubit answers zero, she just writes down zero on her sheet.

00:20:14.296 --> 00:20:19.000
She just has a link between the question she receives and the particle,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:22.624
and she looks at the particle's answer, she writes it down.

00:20:22.624 --> 00:20:26.520
If the question is one, she does not measure the particle directly.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:31.674
She rotates it a little bit, by 45 degrees, before measuring it,

00:20:31.674 --> 00:20:35.689
and she writes down the outcome of the measurement.

00:20:35.689 --> 00:20:39.388
And Bob's strategy is very similar. If his question is zero,

00:20:39.388 --> 00:20:43.739
he rotates his particle by pi over eight, so 22.5 degrees, in the ket zero,

00:20:43.739 --> 00:20:49.000
ket one plane, whatever that means. It's just a transformation on his system that he

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:53.543
does, given that the question is zero, and he replies whatever the qubit has replied.

00:20:53.543 --> 00:20:58.245
And if the question is one, then he rotates it minus pi over eight before measuring and

00:20:58.245 --> 00:20:59.430
writing down the outcome.

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:02.816
If we carry on the calculation, so, an entangled state.

00:21:02.816 --> 00:21:08.685
If the question pair is 00, Alice makes no rotation, Bob makes a rotation by 22.5 degrees,

00:21:08.685 --> 00:21:14.027
then they measure. We realize some statistics, and the statistics of the joint measurements

00:21:14.027 --> 00:21:21.260
will be such that they get 00 with probability 85 divided by two,

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:30.840
11 with probability 85 divided by two, I'm in percent, and therefore they win the CHSH

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.556
game with probability 85 percent and lose on the remaining probability,

00:21:34.556 --> 00:21:40.760
15 percent. So they get to a win rate that is strictly superior to the three-quarter

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:44.752
win rate that we had previously, and they get to 85.

00:21:44.752 --> 00:21:49.120
Correct. Which I guess leads to the question: is there faster than light communication?

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.440
It leads to that question, correct. I like that question.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:58.840
If we've determined that Alice and Bob could not break the three-quarter ceiling,

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.000
how can particles do it?

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:01.500
Yes.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:04.646
How do they work so that they could do this?

00:22:04.646 --> 00:22:05.146
Yes.

00:22:05.146 --> 00:22:09.377
And then a sub-question is, do they communicate faster than light secretly?

00:22:09.377 --> 00:22:13.326
Because we've already established that if they could communicate their questions,

00:22:13.326 --> 00:22:16.977
say, to their partners instantaneously, and maybe their answers,

00:22:16.977 --> 00:22:21.173
then they could organize. The first thing to remark is that it would be a strange conspiracy

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:24.133
that they do that, but they just fulfill an 85 percent win rate.

00:22:24.133 --> 00:22:25.147
You would expect 100.

00:22:25.147 --> 00:22:27.940
You could have, yeah, if they're communicating together.

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:33.560
Yeah. By the way, there's a nice theoretical idealization put forth in the '90s by Sandu

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:37.960
Popescu and Daniel Rohrlich, which is called the Popescu-Rohrlich box,

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.286
which is a non-existent, non-physical device.

00:22:40.286 --> 00:22:44.040
So it doesn't exist, but it's this thought experiment where,

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:48.154
imagine a device that allows you to win the CHSH game 100% of the time.

00:22:48.154 --> 00:22:50.449
Also, that device will not allow communication.

00:22:50.449 --> 00:22:56.567
It looks like one explanation why they could win the CHSH game 100% of the time is communication,

00:22:56.567 --> 00:23:00.887
but to use this as a physical device to communicate, you need to turn this around and

00:23:00.887 --> 00:23:04.338
say, "Okay, what should I do to this PR box, Popescu-Rohrlich box..."

00:23:04.338 --> 00:23:04.838
Yes.

00:23:04.838 --> 00:23:10.208
"...in order to send a bit?" So communication would be, Bob chooses what bit he wants

00:23:10.208 --> 00:23:14.567
to input, and that is the answer of Alice's outcome.

00:23:14.567 --> 00:23:17.680
That would be an example of instantaneous communication.

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:23.301
But the Popescu-Rohrlich box does not allow communication, even though it beats the CHSH inequality.

00:23:23.301 --> 00:23:29.143
And so there's the entangled pairs. Entangled pairs, they do not permit instantaneous communication.

00:23:29.143 --> 00:23:35.160
So there's two aspects here. It's known for everybody who studied basic quantum mechanics,

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:40.962
they can show the so-called non-signaling theorem, which means that whatever Alice does

00:23:40.962 --> 00:23:48.363
to her part of an entangled system, to her particle, there's nothing that will be observable

00:23:48.363 --> 00:23:53.706
on Bob's side after Alice's measurement or after Alice's operation.

00:23:53.706 --> 00:23:56.203
Nothing will be observable on Bob's side.

00:23:56.203 --> 00:23:58.820
So they cannot use that resource to communicate.

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:06.873
But what you alluded to is a different question: maybe how it works is still that they

00:24:06.873 --> 00:24:13.550
secretly communicate behind the scenes, and that would be a way to explain their remarkable

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:20.369
85% winning rate, but yet without giving us macroscopic users the ability to use these

00:24:20.369 --> 00:24:22.431
signaling abilities to communicate faster than light.

00:24:22.431 --> 00:24:27.960
Right. I think we can ponder here, because there's not just the faster than light communication

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:32.920
program that is explicit when we study the CHSH game.

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:38.520
So those who are vocal and say that, "Oh, the conclusion is that nature is non-local,"

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:43.427
what they end up doing is taking very seriously that the only way in which particles

00:24:43.427 --> 00:24:47.598
can really achieve an 85 percent winning rate, because we proved that Alice and Bob could

00:24:47.598 --> 00:24:52.610
not do better than 75, is because they're exchanging information faster than light.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:58.360
Just to clarify, with local and non-local, what we mean is that there's no action at

00:24:58.360 --> 00:25:01.811
a distance, meaning that there's no faster than light communication.

00:25:01.811 --> 00:25:05.080
The last part, meaning that there's no faster than light communication,

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:11.080
is too strong to define locality. Locality concerns what's your story of physics,

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:16.520
and it need not concern your ability to send signals faster than light,

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:19.245
because everybody knows that according to quantum mechanics,

00:25:19.245 --> 00:25:22.120
as I said, it's impossible to send signals faster than light.

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.764
But what's your story of physics matters.

00:25:24.764 --> 00:25:29.292
If your story of physics is that when we have entangled particles,

00:25:29.292 --> 00:25:33.753
when Alice measures her particle, there's a collapse of the wave function.

00:25:33.753 --> 00:25:41.339
The fact that it collapses Alice's system is annoying, but not as annoying as the fact

00:25:41.339 --> 00:25:47.542
that it also collapses Bob's system instantaneously, according to the Copenhagen interpretation.

00:25:47.542 --> 00:25:53.616
When there's a joint wave function for two systems, a collapse is a collapse.

00:25:53.616 --> 00:25:56.186
It doesn't care about these local structures.

00:25:56.186 --> 00:26:00.642
So if one really adopts the worldview of a collapse of the wave function,

00:26:00.642 --> 00:26:05.446
that worldview has a non-local element, in that when Alice measures,

00:26:05.446 --> 00:26:09.560
instantaneously Bob's system has collapsed to one thing.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:10.440
Something happens at a distance.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:14.995
My guess is that the most vocal conclusions about how should we understand violation

00:26:14.995 --> 00:26:23.137
of Bell inequalities is these non-local headlines, probably because it's an obvious solution

00:26:23.137 --> 00:26:29.631
to how to beat the CHSH game that comes to mind, but also because collapse is very ingrained

00:26:29.631 --> 00:26:32.809
in the way we're teaching quantum mechanics.

00:26:32.809 --> 00:26:38.320
It's in every textbook. When there's a measurement, there's a collapse of the wave function,

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:42.868
and then that collapse also affects entangled systems, and therefore it has an effect

00:26:42.868 --> 00:26:48.383
on remote systems. And so non-locality is ingrained in current textbooks via these collapse models.

00:26:48.383 --> 00:26:53.925
I wouldn't even dare to say models, because usually they take the textbook way of presenting collapses.

00:26:53.925 --> 00:26:57.742
It presents it, but it does not really try to study it.

00:26:57.742 --> 00:27:00.140
Yes, it's just a tool, basically.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.686
Yeah, exactly. So we don't exactly know if it's a rule of subjective betting,

00:27:03.686 --> 00:27:07.780
that we should do as if you're an observer and then you've collapsed the system,

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:11.100
or you should take it seriously. Is it really a collapse happening in reality?

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:16.700
And the literature ramifies. There's this most objectivist program which says,

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:21.440
"Oh no, collapse is just a tool." But then obviously you run into these non-realist ontologies

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:28.040
where a theory is just a tool for you, but what are you in physical reality?

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:31.311
And there's also this realist collapse, objective collapse models,

00:27:31.311 --> 00:27:34.620
which are also presented. They bite the bullet on non-locality.

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:38.240
They even declare it's physical. So at least they save realism,

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:41.960
but they have to accommodate this non-locality.

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:42.820
Yes.

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:47.960
Let's not get there for now, 'cause I think we have something else to present.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:52.060
But I want to go back to the idea that because non-locality in collapse has been ingrained,

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:56.111
I think this also contributes to the reason why, if you ask physicists,

00:27:56.111 --> 00:27:58.529
what's the lesson of the 2022 Nobel Prize?

00:27:58.529 --> 00:28:01.592
"Oh, it's because of the non-locality of physics."

00:28:01.592 --> 00:28:02.627
Right.

00:28:02.627 --> 00:28:08.040
This collapse in our textbook mechanism has influenced this meme,

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:12.600
this idea. But I want to pause here because it's a good point to say that at this stage,

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:18.024
when we study how on Earth Alice and Bob with their crystals manage to get 85% winning

00:28:18.024 --> 00:28:24.320
rate, this question also sets the table well to other rival explanations to collapse.

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:29.566
One of them, which has acquired quite a bit of attention recently,

00:28:29.566 --> 00:28:38.240
is superdeterminism. Basically it leverages a true fact, but it leverages it in a way

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:43.500
that becomes extremely annoying for a sound philosophy of physics.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:49.018
So what is the true fact? The fact that when Alice and Bob are initially located on Earth,

00:28:49.018 --> 00:28:54.356
there's actually a process that's happening on Mercury to prepare the question sheet

00:28:54.356 --> 00:28:58.020
for Alice. This question sheet has not been created ex nihilo.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:01.460
It has been created by some physical process.

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:06.383
And there's also, similarly, a physical process happening on Bob's moon of Jupiter.

00:29:06.383 --> 00:29:09.440
And then the point that the superdeterminists make is that, well,

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:14.780
if you go sufficiently far back in time, all of these space-time points,

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:18.666
so Alice and Bob initially sharing randomness or strategies,

00:29:18.666 --> 00:29:24.500
the preparation of Alice's question sheet, preparation of Bob's sheet,

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:28.210
initially, if you go sufficiently back in time, they have a common space-time point.

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:34.690
At least in principle, some mechanism sufficiently far in space-time in the past is responsible

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:40.189
for three things: it's responsible for the questions that will be picked on Alice's side,

00:29:40.189 --> 00:29:45.506
the questions that will be picked on Bob's side, and the way Alice and Bob are organizing

00:29:45.506 --> 00:29:47.562
themselves in order to fulfill the game.

00:29:47.562 --> 00:29:49.922
Completely goes against the notion of free will.

00:29:49.922 --> 00:29:52.020
It goes against the notion of free will.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:56.538
So we're all controlled by this deterministic process at the root of the universe,

00:29:56.538 --> 00:29:57.038
basically.

00:29:57.038 --> 00:30:01.877
Yes, everything spans from that. But that's not the better argument,

00:30:01.877 --> 00:30:07.193
because also in a usually deterministic worldview, the question of free will still arises.

00:30:07.193 --> 00:30:11.930
We often hear that the uppercut for the superdeterministic program is the question of

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:14.615
free will. And then you're like, well, the Schrödinger equation,

00:30:14.615 --> 00:30:20.917
or whatever is your worldview, is also incompatible with a true free will.

00:30:20.917 --> 00:30:23.999
So I don't think that pointing to free will solves the problem.

00:30:23.999 --> 00:30:30.160
Think of it in a scientific way. It would be a conspiratorial worldview of the way systems

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:34.806
and physics is organized. So before going into the microphysics of how this could operate

00:30:34.806 --> 00:30:39.220
physically, I think it's worth going back to what a superdeterministic universe would

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:42.920
mean for Alice and Bob. When they're preparing their strategies,

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:50.420
they already know the questions, or they know a variable which informs them of what the

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:55.540
questions would be. And so that's why they can coordinate themselves in a way that they

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:59.890
achieve a higher win rate, right? Because they know that question number seven will contain

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:02.624
a zero, so on question number seven, "I'm going to output zero,

00:31:02.624 --> 00:31:03.602
you're going to output zero."

00:31:03.602 --> 00:31:05.540
But why they wouldn't take it hundreds?

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:09.700
That's in fact one of the problems that the superdeterminist program faces,

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:13.113
is that there's so many ways to vary their models.

00:31:13.113 --> 00:31:13.613
Yeah.

00:31:13.613 --> 00:31:19.140
And you could vary in so many ways that, how do you actually get to our physics?

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.268
Yes, that's one of the big problems.

00:31:21.268 --> 00:31:26.306
Yeah. But one argument that I made in my book chapter here, which I like,

00:31:26.306 --> 00:31:30.841
is that it's not even worth going back backwards entire, like this variable,

00:31:30.841 --> 00:31:35.607
what would it be. What I like to think is, let's suppose we're making a deal that in

00:31:35.607 --> 00:31:38.491
2027 we're going to do another Bell experiment, a CHSH test,

00:31:38.491 --> 00:31:42.572
with some particles that we're going to be preparing on the very same day,

00:31:42.572 --> 00:31:45.413
because we have to prepare their entanglement and so on.

